---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Jacob A. Torres">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-image">
      <img
        src="/assets/img/red-shirt-picture.jpg"
        alt="Jacob Torres standing outside in a red button-up shirt."
        width="180"
        height="180"
      />
    </div>
    <div class="hero-text">
      <h1>Jacob A. Torres</h1>
      <p>
        I study data science, machine learning, and linguistics at MSU Denver.
        I'm a self-starting tech enthusiast with a passion for language and
        digital accessibility. I also occasionally write short fiction.
      </p>
      <a href="mailto:contact@jacobtorres.net" class="button">Contact me</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="home-features">
    <div class="feature-card">
      <h2>Machine Learning</h2>
      <p>
        Building intelligent applications with Python and data science libraries.
      </p>
      <a href="/projects#machine-learning">View my projects &rarr;</a>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <h2>Web Development</h2>
      <p>
        Building accessible applications with HTML, CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript,
        Django, and Flask.
      </p>
      <a href="/projects#web-development">View my projects &rarr;</a>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <h2>Data Analysis</h2>
      <p>
        Finding the story in public data with pandas, SciPy, and Matplotlib.
      </p>
      <a href="/projects#data-analysis">Read my analysis &rarr;</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="home-section" aria-labelledby="accessibility">
    <h2 id="accessibility">Digital Accessibility</h2>

    <p>
      As a blind tech user, I am very familiar with the frustration of needing
      to use a website or application, only to realize that it doesn't work for
      you because it simply wasn't designed to accommodate your disability.
      Often people only discover that a product is inaccessible after investing
      time, money, and hope. My goal is to eliminate this feeling of
      helplessness and invisibility from the disabled experience.
    </p>

    <p>
      I have been using assistive technology my whole life. In addition to
      reading braille, I'm proficient with NVDA and JAWS on Windows, VoiceOver
      on Mac and iOS, and TalkBack on Android. In college, I helped my school's
      access center evaluate and remediate the accessibility of digital content
      for students with print disabilities. In my world, software has to be
      accessible to the people who build it as well as the people who use it
      &mdash; and developer tooling is still some of the least accessible
      software there is.
    </p>

    <h3>Where AI Comes In</h3>

    <p>
      What interests me most right now is what artificial intelligence can do
      for digital accessibility. Describing an image nobody bothered to label,
      making sense of an unlabeled control, reading a scanned document that no
      screen reader could touch before, navigating an unfamiliar street: these
      were out of reach a few years ago, and they are suddenly plausible. The
      same models that are reshaping everything else happen to be very good at
      exactly the problems that have kept disabled users locked out.
    </p>

    <p>
      I want to explore those possibilities and build the tools that make them
      real. That means treating AI as a way to close the gap rather than widen
      it &mdash; because a technology this useful will become another
      inaccessible default unless disabled developers are the ones shaping it. I
      am actively looking for work and collaborators in this space.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
